\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Recovering the surviving parameters}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{For the grid limits of \ref{fdip-01D4}, the variances at
#{0}, #{e_i}, and #{e_i+e_j} for #{1\le i<j\le n} determine
#{\mu} and #{K}. Here #{e_i} are the standard unit vectors
in the supplied grid coordinates. Explicitly,
##{\begin{aligned}
\mu&=v(0)^{-1},\\
K_{ii}&=\frac{v(e_i)^{-1}-\mu}{4\pi^2},\\
K_{ij}&=\frac{v(e_i+e_j)^{-1}-v(e_i)^{-1}
-v(e_j)^{-1}+\mu}{8\pi^2}\quad(i<j).
\end{aligned}}
Thus two families satisfying that theorem have the same finite
mode limits exactly when their #{\mu,K} agree. This is also
equivalent to equality of their box limits #{\gamma_B} for
every #{B\ge1}. Equality at a single partition is not asserted
to determine the parameters.}

\proof{
\p{The mode variance formula gives the three displayed identities
by expansion of #{(e_i+e_j)\cdot K(e_i+e_j)}. Symmetry determines
the remaining entries. The same formula then proves both directions
for the mode limits, since their centered Gaussian covariances
are specified by those variances. Equal #{\mu,K} give equal
box limits by \ref{fdip-01D6}. It remains to recover modes
from all box limits.}

\p{Fix #{\kappa\in\ZZ^n}. For #{N} divisible by #{B},
form the linear combination of box means
##{b_{B,N,\kappa}=B^{-n}\sum_b
\ue^{-2\pi\ui\kappa\cdot b/B}(A_{B,N}\varphi)(b).}
It is the normalized lattice pairing with the function that is
constant on each box and takes the Fourier character's value at
that box's lower corner. For #{j} in the box labeled #{b},
#{|j_i/N-b_i/B|\le 1/B}, so
##{|\ue^{-2\pi\ui\kappa\cdot j/N}
-\ue^{-2\pi\ui\kappa\cdot b/B}|
\le\frac{2\pi|\kappa|_1}{B}.}
The covariance of #{\varphi} is #{N^nQ_N^{-1}}, and
#{Q_N^{-1}\le(2/\mu)\bbI}. Applying this operator inequality
to the difference of the two lattice test functions gives
##{\mathbb E|b_{B,N,\kappa}-a_{N,\kappa}|^2
\le\frac2\mu\Bigl(\frac{2\pi|\kappa|_1}{B}\Bigr)^2.}
The calculation uses the complexified counting pairing and holds
for real and imaginary parts together.}

\p{For fixed #{B}, convergence of box covariances gives a limit
#{w_B(\kappa)} of #{\mathbb E|b_{B,N,\kappa}|^2}; it is
determined by #{\gamma_B}. The triangle inequality in Gaussian
mean square gives
##{|\sqrt{w_B(\kappa)}-\sqrt{v(\kappa)}|
\le\sqrt{\frac2\mu}\frac{2\pi|\kappa|_1}{B},}
after letting #{N\to\infty} through multiples of #{B}.
Thus the collection of box laws determines #{v(\kappa)}
by letting #{B} grow. Equal box laws for two families force
equal mode variances, even if their initial mass parameters
were different; the separate error bounds both tend to zero.
The displayed reconstruction formulas finish the proof.}
}

\p{For #{B=1}, the only box mean is the constant Fourier mode.
Its limiting variance is #{1/\mu}, independent of #{K}. This
explains why the assertion uses all box partitions.}
